const extraRows=`
rhind|Rhind mathematical papyrus|-1650|Mathematics|ahmes:Ahmes|rhind|Ancient Egypt|c.|A surviving mathematical manuscript copied by the scribe Ahmes records practical arithmetic and geometry. It preserves older knowledge; it does not identify a single inventor of mathematics.
babylon|Babylonian numerical astronomy|-700|Astronomy||math|Mesopotamia|c.|Generations of observers recorded celestial cycles and developed numerical prediction. Collective records, rather than a single named scientist, underpin this tradition.
euclid-elements|Geometry from axioms|-300|Mathematics|euclid:Euclid|euclid|Hellenistic Egypt|c.|The Elements organized earlier mathematics into definitions, postulates and proofs. Its influence extended through translations and education for many centuries.
archimedes-lever|Levers & equilibrium|-250|Mechanics|archimedes:Archimedes|archimedes|Syracuse|c.|Archimedes developed mathematical treatments of equilibrium and centres of gravity. These works helped make mechanics a subject of proof as well as practical construction.
archimedes-buoyancy|Mathematics of buoyancy|-250|Mechanics|archimedes|archimedes|Syracuse|c.|On Floating Bodies treated the balance between weight and displaced fluid. The familiar bath story is a later anecdote, not the evidence for the scientific result.
earth-size|Measuring Earth’s circumference|-240|Astronomy|eratosthenes:Eratosthenes|eratosthenes|Hellenistic Egypt|c.|Eratosthenes combined geometry, distances and solar angles to estimate Earth’s circumference. The exact accuracy depends on the ancient length unit used.
ptolemy-model|A predictive geocentric model|150|Astronomy|ptolemy:Claudius Ptolemy|ptolemy|Roman Egypt|c.|The Almagest synthesized mathematical astronomy into a model centred on Earth. Although later replaced, it offered useful predictions and shaped astronomy for centuries.
liu-commentary|Proofs in the Nine Chapters|263|Mathematics|liuhui:Liu Hui|liu|China||Liu Hui’s commentary explained algorithms and geometry in an influential Chinese mathematical text. It included a polygon method for approximating pi.
aryabhatiya|The Aryabhatiya|499|Mathematics|aryabhata:Aryabhata|aryabhata|India||Aryabhata summarized mathematics and astronomy, including sine tables and calculation methods. He explained apparent celestial motion through Earth’s axial rotation.
zero|Arithmetic with zero|628|Mathematics|brahmagupta:Brahmagupta|brahmagupta|India||Brahmagupta set out rules for zero and negative numbers. Some rules, particularly division by zero, differ from modern mathematics; this was an important stage of development.
algebra|Systematic algebra|820|Mathematics|khwarizmi:Muhammad ibn Musa al Khwarizmi|khwarizmi|Abbasid Baghdad|c.|A treatise organized methods for solving linear and quadratic equations. The word algebra derives from its title; later translations carried these methods into new scholarly traditions.
optics-book|The Book of Optics|1021|Mechanics|haytham:Ibn al Haytham|haytham|Fatimid Egypt|c.|Ibn al Haytham combined geometry and experiments to study light and vision, arguing that vision results from light entering the eye.
biruni-earth|Earth’s radius from a mountain|1030|Astronomy|biruni:Al Biruni|biruni|Central & South Asia|c.|Al Biruni described a geometrical method for estimating Earth’s radius using a mountain height and the angle to the horizon.
fibonacci-numerals|Liber Abaci & numeral transmission|1202|Mathematics|fibonacci:Leonardo of Pisa|fibonacci|Italy & North Africa||Fibonacci presented calculation using Hindu Arabic numerals to Latin readers. Their development and transmission involved many earlier Indian and Islamic scholars.
kerala-series|Infinite series in Kerala|1400|Mathematics|madhava:Madhava of Sangamagrama|madhava|India|c.|The Kerala school developed series for trigonometric functions and pi, with results attributed to Madhava in later writings. A direct transmission to European calculus is not established here.
heliocentric|A Sun centred planetary system|1543|Astronomy|copernicus:Nicolaus Copernicus|planetary|Poland||Copernicus published a mathematical planetary system in which Earth moves around the Sun. The model retained circular motions and was refined by later astronomers.
anatomy|Anatomy through dissection|1543|Medicine|vesalius:Andreas Vesalius|vesalius|Europe||Vesalius published a detailed anatomy based on human dissection, challenging errors inherited from ancient medical authorities.
magnet-earth|Earth as a magnet|1600|Electricity|gilbert:William Gilbert|electric|England||Gilbert’s De Magnete investigated magnetic phenomena and argued that Earth behaves as a magnet. His experimental approach helped distinguish magnetic and electrical effects.
ellipses|Elliptical planetary orbits|1609|Astronomy|kepler:Johannes Kepler;brahe:Tycho Brahe|kepler|Central Europe||Kepler used Brahe’s precise observations to formulate his first two laws of planetary motion. Ellipses replaced the circular orbit assumption.
jupiter|Moons beyond Earth|1610|Astronomy|galileo:Galileo Galilei|planetary|Italy||Galileo’s telescopic observations of Jupiter’s moons showed that not everything orbits Earth, adding evidence against a strict geocentric system.
logarithms|Logarithms simplify calculation|1614|Mathematics|napier:John Napier|math|Scotland||Napier published logarithms, allowing difficult multiplication and division to be replaced by addition and subtraction. Tables became important tools in astronomy and navigation.
planet-laws|A third law of planetary motion|1619|Astronomy|kepler|kepler|Central Europe||Kepler related a planet’s orbital period to the size of its orbit. Newton later explained these regularities within a gravitational theory.
circulation|Circulation of the blood|1628|Medicine|harvey:William Harvey|medicine|England||Harvey argued from observation and calculation that the heart circulates blood in a closed system, revising older accounts of blood production and flow.
analytic-geometry|Coordinates connect algebra & geometry|1637|Mathematics|descartes:René Descartes;fermat:Pierre de Fermat|descartes|France||Descartes and Fermat developed ways to study curves through equations. Their approaches connected geometric problems with algebraic calculation.
motion|Mathematical laws of falling bodies|1638|Mechanics|galileo|newton|Italy||Galileo’s Two New Sciences developed mathematical accounts of motion and material strength. Its treatment of acceleration became part of the foundations of later mechanics.
probability|A mathematical theory of chance|1654|Mathematics|pascal:Blaise Pascal;fermat|fermat|France||Correspondence between Pascal and Fermat analysed games of chance, helping establish probability as a mathematical subject rather than intuition alone.
gas-law|Pressure & volume in gases|1662|Chemistry|boyle:Robert Boyle|lavoisier|England||Boyle reported the inverse relation between pressure and volume for a gas under suitable conditions. Gas experiments helped make chemistry increasingly quantitative.
cells|A name for cells|1665|Biology|hooke:Robert Hooke|genomes|England||Hooke’s Micrographia described tiny compartments in cork and called them cells. The observation preceded understanding of living cells and their internal structures.
microbes|A microscopic living world|1676|Biology|leeuwenhoek:Antonie van Leeuwenhoek|medicine|Dutch Republic||Using powerful single lens microscopes, Leeuwenhoek described microorganisms. His observations opened a previously unseen scale of life to investigation.
calculus|Calculus becomes a general method|1684|Mathematics|newton:Isaac Newton;leibniz:Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz|leibniz|Europe||Leibniz published differential calculus in 1684; Newton had developed related methods earlier. Their independent approaches became central tools for changing quantities and motion.
gravity|Universal gravitation|1687|Mechanics|newton|newton|England||The Principia united terrestrial and celestial motion through laws of motion and gravitation. It mathematically explained important features of Kepler’s planetary laws.
classification|A systematic naming of life|1735|Biology|linnaeus:Carl Linnaeus|genomes|Sweden||Systema Naturae helped organize biological classification. Linnaeus developed naming conventions that later became standard, while classifications themselves continued to change.
oxygen|Oxygen investigated|1774|Chemistry|priestley:Joseph Priestley;scheele:Carl Wilhelm Scheele|priestley|England & Sweden||Priestley and Scheele independently prepared oxygen around this period. Their interpretations differed from Lavoisier’s later explanation of combustion.
combustion|An oxygen theory of combustion|1777|Chemistry|lavoisier:Antoine Lavoisier|lavoisier|France||Lavoisier used measurements to explain combustion through oxygen, challenging the phlogiston theory. The chemical revolution involved collaborators as well as his own experiments.
herschel|Discovery of Uranus|1781|Astronomy|wherschel:William Herschel;cherschel:Caroline Herschel|hubble|Britain||William Herschel identified a new planet. Caroline’s assistance and later independent astronomical work formed part of the Herschel observing programme.
deep-time|Earth has a deep history|1785|Earth science|hutton:James Hutton|geology|Scotland||Hutton argued that geological processes operating over immense time could explain Earth’s rocks. His approach challenged short chronologies of Earth’s past.
chemistry-elements|A new language for chemistry|1789|Chemistry|lavoisier;marieanne:Marie Anne Paulze Lavoisier|lavoisier|France||Lavoisier’s chemistry textbook organized substances and quantitative principles. Marie Anne contributed illustrations, translation and laboratory documentation to their scientific work.
vaccination|Smallpox vaccination|1796|Medicine|jenner:Edward Jenner|medicine|England||Jenner tested protection against smallpox using material associated with cowpox. The work built on earlier inoculation practices rather than inventing immunization from nothing.
battery|A sustained source of current|1800|Electricity|volta:Alessandro Volta|davy|Italy||Volta’s pile supplied sustained electric current. Batteries enabled new electrochemical experiments and investigations of electromagnetism.
interference|Light shows interference|1801|Mechanics|young:Thomas Young|waves|England||Young developed an interference account of light, supporting its wave character. Later quantum physics would revisit the relationship between waves and particles.
atomic-theory|A quantitative atomic theory|1803|Chemistry|dalton:John Dalton|dalton|England||Dalton proposed that elements consist of atoms and compounds combine them in characteristic proportions, helping explain chemical regularities.
electrolysis|Electricity isolates new elements|1807|Chemistry|davy|davy|England||Davy used electrolysis to isolate potassium and sodium. Volta’s battery made it possible to separate substances that chemical methods had not isolated.
avogadro|Equal gas volumes & molecules|1811|Chemistry|avogadro:Amedeo Avogadro|periodic|Italy||Avogadro proposed that equal gas volumes at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules, clarifying distinctions between atoms and molecules.
ampere|Currents exert magnetic forces|1820|Electricity|ampere:André Marie Ampère|electric|France||Ampère investigated forces between current carrying conductors after Ørsted’s discovery, developing a quantitative approach to electrodynamics.
heat-engine|Limits of heat engines|1824|Mechanics|carnot:Sadi Carnot|climate|France||Carnot studied how heat engines operate and the limits of converting heat into work. His reasoning preceded the mature laws of thermodynamics.
atmosphere|The atmosphere & Earth’s warmth|1824|Climate|fourier:Joseph Fourier|climate|France||Fourier reasoned that the atmosphere affects Earth’s temperature. The modern greenhouse explanation developed through later experiments and radiation theory.
ohm|Resistance, voltage & current|1827|Electricity|ohm:Georg Simon Ohm|electric|German states||Ohm formulated a quantitative relation between current, potential difference and resistance for suitable conductors, helping make circuit behaviour calculable.
urea-synthesis|An organic compound in the laboratory|1828|Chemistry|wohler:Friedrich Wöhler|urea|German states||Wöhler prepared urea from inorganic starting material. The experiment challenged simple boundaries between living and nonliving chemistry, without ending vitalism overnight.
geology-principles|Principles of Geology|1830|Earth science|lyell:Charles Lyell|geology|Britain||Lyell developed an influential account of geological change through observable processes. His work shaped Darwin’s thinking about time and gradual change.
analytical-engine|A programmable mechanical engine|1837|Computing|babbage:Charles Babbage|babbage|England||Babbage designed the Analytical Engine, a proposed general purpose mechanical computer. A complete machine was not built in his lifetime.
cell-theory|Cells as units of life|1839|Biology|schleiden:Matthias Schleiden;schwann:Theodor Schwann|genomes|German states||Schleiden and Schwann generalized cellular organization across plants and animals. Later work revised their account of how new cells arise.
ada-notes|An algorithm & a broader idea of computing|1843|Computing|ada:Ada Lovelace;babbage|babbage|England||Lovelace’s notes on the Analytical Engine included a method for Bernoulli numbers and envisioned manipulating symbols beyond arithmetic. Early programming credit has a complex history.
energy|Heat & mechanical work|1843|Mechanics|joule:James Prescott Joule|climate|England||Joule measured relationships between mechanical work and heat. His experiments contributed to the principle of energy conservation alongside other researchers’ work.
anesthesia|Public demonstration of ether anesthesia|1846|Medicine|morton:William T. G. Morton;warren:John Collins Warren|medicine|United States||A public surgical demonstration helped establish ether anesthesia. Earlier experiments and other practitioners make single inventor accounts misleading.
hygiene|Handwashing reduces puerperal fever|1847|Medicine|semmelweis:Ignaz Semmelweis|medicine|Austrian Empire||Semmelweis observed that a cleansing procedure greatly reduced deaths in a maternity clinic. A convincing microbial explanation and broad acceptance came later.
second-law|A second law of thermodynamics|1850|Mechanics|clausius:Rudolf Clausius;kelvin:William Thomson, Lord Kelvin|climate|Europe||Clausius and Kelvin developed accounts of the direction and limits of heat processes. Clausius later introduced entropy in 1865.
cholera|Mapping cholera & contaminated water|1854|Medicine|snow:John Snow|medicine|England||Snow connected a London cholera outbreak to a contaminated water source through mapping and investigation, strengthening an evidence based approach to public health.
non-euclidean|Geometry beyond Euclid|1854|Mathematics|riemann:Bernhard Riemann;lobachevsky:Nikolai Lobachevsky;bolyai:János Bolyai|riemann|Europe||Riemann broadened the study of curved spaces, following earlier non Euclidean geometry. These mathematical tools later helped express general relativity.
cell-division|Cells arise from cells|1855|Biology|virchow:Rudolf Virchow;remak:Robert Remak|genomes|German states||Research on cell division replaced older ideas of free cell formation. Virchow popularized a principle supported by Remak’s earlier work.
evolution|Evolution by natural selection|1858|Biology|darwin:Charles Darwin;wallace:Alfred Russel Wallace|genomes|Britain & Southeast Asia||Darwin and Wallace’s ideas were presented together in 1858. Darwin’s 1859 book developed the argument at length; inheritance mechanisms were still unresolved.
infrared-gases|Gases absorb infrared radiation|1859|Climate|tyndall:John Tyndall;foote:Eunice Newton Foote|climate|Britain & United States||Tyndall measured absorption by gases; Foote had reported gas warming experiments in 1856. Their different experiments contributed to the history of atmospheric warming.
mendel|Patterns of inheritance|1866|Genetics|mendel:Gregor Mendel|genomes|Austrian Empire||Mendel published pea breeding experiments describing regularities in inheritance. Later scientists connected these patterns with chromosomes and molecular mechanisms.
antisepsis|Antiseptic surgery|1867|Medicine|lister:Joseph Lister|medicine|Britain||Lister applied ideas about microorganisms to surgical infection control. His antiseptic procedures reduced infections and helped change surgical practice.
periodic-table|Periodicity among elements|1869|Chemistry|mendeleev:Dmitri Mendeleev;meyer:Julius Lothar Meyer|periodic|Russia & German states||Mendeleev and Meyer developed periodic classifications. Mendeleev used recurring properties to predict missing elements, helping distinguish a predictive system from a simple list.
dna-isolated|Isolation of nuclein|1869|Genetics|miescher:Friedrich Miescher|genomes|Switzerland & Germany||Miescher isolated a phosphorus rich substance from cell nuclei, later called DNA. Its role in inheritance was not yet understood.
statistical-mechanics|Entropy & molecular statistics|1877|Mechanics|boltzmann:Ludwig Boltzmann|climate|Austria||Boltzmann related macroscopic thermodynamic behaviour to molecular states and probabilities. Statistical mechanics bridged atoms and everyday properties of matter.
germ-proof|Microorganisms & specific diseases|1882|Medicine|koch:Robert Koch;pasteur:Louis Pasteur|medicine|Germany & France||Koch identified the tuberculosis bacterium. His and Pasteur’s wider research strengthened the case that microorganisms cause particular diseases.
rabies|A vaccine against rabies|1885|Medicine|pasteur;roux:Émile Roux|medicine|France||Pasteur’s team developed an approach to rabies vaccination. Vaccine research joined laboratory microbiology with prevention in patients.
michelson|A test for the ether wind|1887|Mechanics|michelson:Albert A. Michelson;morley:Edward Morley|relativity|United States||An interferometer experiment did not find the expected effect of Earth’s motion through a light carrying ether. Its place in relativity’s development is subtler than a single decisive cause.
xray|X rays reveal hidden structures|1895|Atoms|roentgen:Wilhelm Röntgen|roentgen|Germany||Röntgen discovered penetrating radiation that could image internal structures. X rays later became tools for both medicine and crystallography.
co2-warming|Calculating CO₂ driven warming|1896|Climate|arrhenius:Svante Arrhenius|climateco2|Sweden||Arrhenius calculated how changes in atmospheric carbon dioxide could affect temperature. Modern estimates use far more complete physics and data.
malaria-mosquito|Mosquitoes & malaria transmission|1897|Medicine|ross:Ronald Ross|malaria|British India||Ross found malaria parasites in mosquitoes and studied transmission in birds. Other researchers subsequently established important parts of human malaria transmission.
quantum-energy|The quantum of action|1900|Quantum|planck|quantum|Germany||Planck’s radiation work introduced the constant relating quantum energy to frequency, opening a route toward a new microscopic physics.
chromosome|Chromosomes & inheritance|1902|Genetics|sutton:Walter Sutton;boveri:Theodor Boveri|genomes|United States & Germany||Sutton and Boveri connected chromosome behaviour with inheritance. Their separate work helped give Mendel’s patterns a cellular basis.
special-relativity|Special relativity|1905|Mechanics|einstein;lorentz:Hendrik Antoon Lorentz;poincare:Henri Poincaré|relativity|Europe||Einstein formulated a theory reconciling motion with a constant light speed. Earlier work by Lorentz and Poincaré formed part of the broader intellectual setting.
brownian|Brownian motion & evidence for atoms|1905|Atoms|einstein;perrin:Jean Perrin|dalton|Europe||Einstein quantitatively explained the random motion of suspended particles through molecular collisions. Perrin’s later experiments strengthened evidence for the reality of atoms.
enzyme-fermentation|Fermentation without intact cells|1897|Biology|buchner:Eduard Buchner|enzyme|Germany||Buchner showed that cell extracts can ferment sugar, demonstrating that biochemical activity need not require an intact living cell.
superconductivity|Electrical resistance disappears|1911|Quantum|onnes:Heike Kamerlingh Onnes|superconduct|Netherlands||Onnes observed the disappearance of electrical resistance in sufficiently cold mercury. A detailed microscopic explanation emerged decades later.
cepheids|Cepheids as cosmic distance markers|1912|Astronomy|leavitt:Henrietta Swan Leavitt|leavitt|United States||Leavitt identified a relation between a Cepheid’s pulsation period and brightness. Calibration turned the relation into an important astronomical distance tool.
continental-drift|Continents can move|1912|Earth science|wegener:Alfred Wegener|geology|Germany||Wegener assembled evidence for continental drift. His proposed mechanisms were inadequate; later seafloor research supported motion within plate tectonics.
xray-crystals|Reading crystals with X rays|1912|Chemistry|laue:Max von Laue;whbragg:William Henry Bragg;wlbragg:William Lawrence Bragg|crystal|Europe||Laue’s diffraction experiments and the Braggs’ methods made atomic arrangements in crystals measurable, laying a foundation for structural chemistry and biology.
atomic-number|Atomic number orders the elements|1913|Chemistry|moseley:Henry Moseley|periodic|Britain||Moseley’s X ray measurements linked element ordering to nuclear charge, clarifying why atomic number is more fundamental than atomic mass in the periodic table.
ramanujan-partitions|New mathematics of partitions|1918|Mathematics|ramanujan:Srinivasa Ramanujan;hardy:G. H. Hardy|ramanujan|India & Britain||Ramanujan and Hardy developed an asymptotic formula for the number of integer partitions, combining remarkable insight with rigorous analytic methods.
general-relativity|Gravity as spacetime geometry|1915|Mechanics|einstein;grossmann:Marcel Grossmann|relativity|Europe||Einstein’s gravitational theory describes gravity through spacetime geometry. Mathematical tools and earlier collaboration with Grossmann helped make the formulation possible.
noether-symmetry|Symmetry & conservation laws|1918|Mathematics|noether:Emmy Noether|noether|Germany||Noether established a deep relation between continuous symmetries and conservation laws, connecting abstract mathematics to physical quantities such as energy and momentum.
ammonia|Ammonia synthesized from its elements|1909|Chemistry|haber:Fritz Haber;bosch:Carl Bosch|haber|Germany||Haber demonstrated ammonia synthesis from nitrogen and hydrogen; Bosch helped develop industrial production. The process transformed fertilizer supply and also supported explosives manufacture.
insulin-discovery|Insulin becomes a treatment|1921|Medicine|banting:Frederick Banting;best:Charles Best;macleod:J. J. R. Macleod;collip:James Collip|insulin|Canada||The Toronto team isolated and refined pancreatic extracts for treating diabetes. The first successful patient treatment followed in 1922; the work involved all four researchers.
compton-effect|X ray scattering & light quanta|1923|Quantum|compton:Arthur Compton|compton|United States||Compton measured a wavelength change when X rays scatter from electrons, supplying evidence for a particle description of light.
matter-waves|Particles have wave properties|1924|Quantum|debroglie:Louis de Broglie|debroglie|France||De Broglie proposed that matter has associated waves. Later electron diffraction experiments tested this new connection between wave and particle descriptions.
other-galaxies|Andromeda lies beyond the Milky Way|1924|Astronomy|hubble:Edwin Hubble|hubble|United States||Hubble used Cepheid observations to show that Andromeda is a separate galaxy, greatly enlarging the known scale of the universe.
stellar-composition|Stars are mainly hydrogen & helium|1925|Astronomy|payne:Cecilia Payne Gaposchkin|cosmos|United States||Payne used spectroscopy and ionization theory to infer stellar composition. Her thesis challenged the assumption that stars share Earth’s elemental proportions.
matrix-mechanics|Quantum mechanics with matrices|1925|Quantum|heisenberg:Werner Heisenberg;born:Max Born;jordan:Pascual Jordan|heisenberg|Germany||Heisenberg’s reformulation and work by Born and Jordan expressed atomic physics through matrices, helping establish a new quantum mechanics.
exclusion|The exclusion principle|1925|Quantum|pauli:Wolfgang Pauli|pauli|Europe||Pauli proposed a restriction on identical fermions occupying the same quantum state, helping explain atomic structure and the organization of electrons.
wave-equation|Wave mechanics|1926|Quantum|schrodinger:Erwin Schrödinger|schrodinger|Switzerland||Schrödinger developed a wave equation for quantum systems. Wave mechanics proved equivalent to the matrix approach despite their different mathematical appearances.
probability-wave|A probabilistic meaning for the wave|1926|Quantum|born|born|Germany||Born interpreted the quantum wavefunction in terms of probabilities of measurement outcomes, changing what the mathematical theory predicts about experiments.
electron-diffraction|Electron diffraction|1927|Quantum|davisson:Clinton Davisson;germer:Lester Germer;gpthomson:George Paget Thomson|electron-diff|United States & Britain||Independent experiments demonstrated wave behaviour of electrons, supplying experimental support for matter waves.
uncertainty|Limits on paired measurements|1927|Quantum|heisenberg|heisenberg|Germany||Heisenberg established an uncertainty relation for quantities such as position and momentum. It is a feature of quantum theory rather than merely imperfect instruments.
expanding-model|An expanding universe model|1927|Astronomy|lemaitre:Georges Lemaître;friedmann:Alexander Friedmann|cosmos|Belgium & Soviet Union||Lemaître connected expanding relativistic models with galaxy observations. Friedmann had already explored nonstatic solutions to Einstein’s equations in 1922.
raman-scattering|Light changes colour when scattered|1928|Quantum|raman:C. V. Raman;krishnan:K. S. Krishnan|raman|India||Raman and Krishnan reported scattering with changed frequency, revealing exchanges of energy between light and molecules and enabling a powerful spectroscopic method.
dirac-electron|A relativistic equation for the electron|1928|Quantum|dirac:Paul Dirac|dirac|Britain||Dirac combined quantum mechanics with special relativity for the electron. The theory led toward the prediction of antimatter.
penicillin-observed|The antibacterial effect of penicillin|1928|Medicine|fleming:Alexander Fleming|penicillin|Britain||Fleming observed that a mould could inhibit bacteria. Turning this observation into a reliable treatment required later biochemical and clinical work.
transformation|Bacterial transformation|1928|Genetics|griffith:Frederick Griffith|avery|Britain||Griffith found that material from killed bacteria could change living bacteria. The chemical identity of the transforming material was not yet known.
redshift-distance|Galaxy recession & distance|1929|Astronomy|hubble;humason:Milton Humason;slipher:Vesto Slipher|hubble|United States||Hubble published a relation between galaxy distances and radial velocities, drawing on a growing body of observations. The expansion story also includes Slipher and Lemaître.
incompleteness|Limits of formal proof|1931|Mathematics|godel:Kurt Gödel|godel|Austria||Gödel showed limits on sufficiently expressive formal mathematical systems. The result reshaped ambitions to capture all mathematical truths in a single formal framework.
cyclotron|Accelerating particles in a spiral|1931|Atoms|lawrence:Ernest Lawrence;livingston:M. Stanley Livingston|lawrence|United States||The cyclotron used magnetic fields and alternating electric fields to accelerate particles repeatedly, enabling new nuclear experiments.
positron|Discovery of the positron|1932|Atoms|anderson:Carl Anderson|anderson|United States||Anderson identified a positively charged counterpart of the electron in cosmic ray observations, giving experimental evidence for antimatter.
neutron-irradiation|Nuclei respond to neutron irradiation|1934|Atoms|fermi:Enrico Fermi|fermi|Italy||Fermi’s team investigated nuclear reactions induced by neutrons and the special effectiveness of slow neutrons, expanding nuclear research.
turing-machine|A mathematical model of computation|1936|Computing|turing:Alan Turing;church:Alonzo Church|computing|Britain & United States||Turing formalized computation using an abstract machine while Church developed a different formal approach. Their work clarified what can and cannot be algorithmically computed.
fission|Nuclear fission identified & explained|1938|Atoms|hahn:Otto Hahn;strassmann:Fritz Strassmann;meitner:Lise Meitner;frisch:Otto Frisch|fermi|Germany & Scandinavia||Hahn and Strassmann found chemical evidence of uranium splitting. Meitner and Frisch interpreted the result as nuclear fission in early 1939.
penicillin-treatment|Penicillin developed for therapy|1940|Medicine|florey:Howard Florey;chain:Ernst Chain;heatley:Norman Heatley|penicillin|Britain||The Oxford team developed methods to extract and test penicillin. Animal studies and later clinical use transformed Fleming’s observation into a practical medicine.
chain-reaction|A controlled nuclear chain reaction|1942|Atoms|fermi;szilard:Leó Szilárd|fermi|United States||Chicago Pile 1 achieved a controlled self sustaining fission chain reaction through a large collaborative effort, opening possibilities for both nuclear energy and weapons.
dna-transform|DNA as transforming material|1944|Genetics|avery:Oswald Avery;macLeod:Colin MacLeod;mccarty:Maclyn McCarty|avery|United States||Avery, MacLeod and McCarty identified DNA as the material responsible for bacterial transformation, providing evidence for its genetic role.
protein-crystal|Structure of penicillin|1945|Chemistry|hodgkin:Dorothy Crowfoot Hodgkin|hodgkin|Britain||Hodgkin and collaborators used X ray crystallography to solve penicillin’s structure, helping connect chemical function with atomic arrangement.
stored-program|Architecture for a stored program|1945|Computing|vonneumann:John von Neumann;eckert:J. Presper Eckert;mauchly:John Mauchly|computing|United States||A widely circulated draft described a computer storing instructions and data in memory. The architecture developed through collective discussions, so naming it after one person understates the work.
transistor-point|The point contact transistor|1947|Computing|bardeen:John Bardeen;brattain:Walter Brattain;shockley:William Shockley|transistor|United States||Bardeen and Brattain demonstrated a transistor at Bell Labs. Shockley’s related junction transistor work followed, helping make practical semiconductor electronics possible.
information|A mathematical theory of information|1948|Computing|shannon:Claude Shannon|computing|United States||Shannon provided a mathematical framework for information, noise and communication limits, underpinning modern digital communication.
quantum-electrodynamics|Quantum electrodynamics refined|1948|Quantum|feynman:Richard Feynman;schwinger:Julian Schwinger;tomonaga:Sin Itiro Tomonaga;dyson:Freeman Dyson|qed|United States & Japan||Different approaches to quantum electrodynamics were made consistent and experimentally powerful. Dyson clarified connections between methods developed by Feynman, Schwinger and Tomonaga.
mobile-genes|Genes can change position|1950|Genetics|mcclintock:Barbara McClintock|transposons|United States||McClintock’s maize studies showed genetic elements moving within chromosomes, revealing a genome more dynamic than earlier models suggested.
dna-virus|DNA carries viral genetic instructions|1952|Genetics|hershey:Alfred Hershey;chase:Martha Chase|hershey|United States||The Hershey Chase experiment traced DNA and protein during bacterial infection, strengthening evidence that DNA is genetic material.
nerve-signals|Electrical mechanisms of nerve signals|1952|Biology|hodgkin-nerve:Alan Hodgkin;huxley:Andrew Huxley|nerve|Britain||Measurements and a mathematical model explained action potentials through changing ion conductances across a nerve membrane.
polio|A vaccine against polio|1955|Medicine|salk:Jonas Salk;sabin:Albert Sabin|medicine|United States||Salk’s inactivated vaccine was shown effective in a large trial. Sabin later developed an oral live attenuated vaccine; the approaches differ rather than represent one invention.
insulin-sequence|The sequence of a protein|1955|Biology|sanger:Frederick Sanger|sangerprotein|Britain||Sanger established the amino acid sequence of insulin, showing that a protein can have a precisely determined chemical sequence.
neutrino-detect|Detecting the neutrino|1956|Atoms|reines:Frederick Reines;cowan:Clyde Cowan|neutrino|United States||Reines and Cowan detected antineutrinos from a reactor, observing a particle proposed to account for missing energy in beta decay.
parity|Parity symmetry fails|1956|Atoms|wu:Chien Shiung Wu;lee:Tsung Dao Lee;yang:Chen Ning Yang|wu|United States||Lee and Yang questioned parity conservation in weak interactions. Wu’s experiment, completed across 1956 and 1957, demonstrated its violation.
bcs-theory|A microscopic theory of superconductivity|1957|Quantum|bardeen;cooper:Leon Cooper;schrieffer:John Robert Schrieffer|bcs|United States||BCS theory explained conventional superconductivity through correlated electron pairs and a collective quantum state.
myoglobin|A protein’s three dimensional structure|1958|Biology|kendrew:John Kendrew;perutz:Max Perutz|proteinshape|Britain||Kendrew’s myoglobin work and Perutz’s later haemoglobin structure established atomic models of globular proteins through crystallography.
keeling-curve|Measuring rising atmospheric CO₂|1958|Climate|keeling:Charles David Keeling|climateco2|United States||Precise monitoring at Mauna Loa and other sites revealed seasonal cycles and a long term rise in atmospheric carbon dioxide.
integrated-circuit|Several components on one chip|1958|Computing|kilby:Jack Kilby;noyce:Robert Noyce|chip|United States||Kilby demonstrated an integrated circuit in 1958. Noyce’s independently developed planar approach in 1959 supported practical silicon integration.
laser-light|A working laser|1960|Engineering|maiman:Theodore Maiman;townes:Charles Townes;schawlow:Arthur Schawlow|laser|United States||Maiman demonstrated a ruby laser. Maser research and optical proposals by Townes, Schawlow and others supplied important groundwork.
mrna|Messenger RNA connects gene & protein|1961|Genetics|brenner:Sydney Brenner;jacob:François Jacob;meselson:Matthew Meselson|geneticcode|Britain & United States||Experiments established a short lived RNA intermediary carrying genetic information to protein making machinery. Separate research groups contributed to identifying messenger RNA.
first-codon|Reading the first codon|1961|Genetics|nirenberg:Marshall Nirenberg;matthaei:Heinrich Matthaei|geneticcode|United States||A synthetic RNA experiment linked a repeated RNA sequence to phenylalanine, providing a first foothold in deciphering the genetic code.
seafloor|Seafloor spreading|1962|Earth science|hess:Harry Hess;dietz:Robert Dietz|plates|United States||Proposals for new ocean crust forming at ridges offered a mechanism for moving ocean floors and contributed to the eventual plate tectonic framework.
magnetic-stripes|Magnetic stripes record ocean history|1963|Earth science|vine:Frederick Vine;matthews:Drummond Matthews;morleygeo:Lawrence Morley|plates|Britain & Canada||Magnetic reversal patterns provided a testable explanation of seafloor spreading. Vine, Matthews and Morley developed related ideas independently.
quark-model|A model built from quarks|1964|Atoms|gellmann:Murray Gell Mann;zweig:George Zweig|quarks|United States||Gell Mann and Zweig independently proposed quark based accounts of hadrons, simplifying patterns among the growing collection of particles.
higgs-mechanism|A mechanism for particle masses|1964|Atoms|higgs:Peter Higgs;englert:François Englert;brout:Robert Brout;kibble:Tom Kibble;guralnik:Gerald Guralnik;hagen:Carl Hagen|higgs|Europe & United States||Several groups developed mechanisms of spontaneous symmetry breaking in gauge theories. The Higgs boson became an important experimental test of this framework.
cosmic-background|The cosmic microwave background|1965|Astronomy|penzias:Arno Penzias;wilsonastro:Robert Wilson;dicke:Robert Dicke;peebles:James Peebles|cmb|United States||Penzias and Wilson detected microwave radiation; a Princeton group supplied its cosmological interpretation. The background supports a hot early universe.
transform-faults|Transform faults link plate motions|1965|Earth science|tuzowilson:J. Tuzo Wilson|wilson|Canada||Wilson introduced transform faults, explaining how different plate boundaries connect and how their motion differs from ordinary faults.
genetic-code|Deciphering the genetic code|1966|Genetics|nirenberg;khorana:Har Gobind Khorana;holley:Robert Holley|geneticcode|United States||Multiple experiments established how RNA codons specify amino acids. Nirenberg, Khorana and Holley made major contributions to decoding and understanding the system.
plate-tectonics|A unified theory of plate tectonics|1967|Earth science|mckenzie:Dan McKenzie;parker:Robert Parker;morgan:W. Jason Morgan|plates|Britain & United States||Researchers formulated global plate motion models that united seafloor spreading, earthquakes and continental movement. The framework emerged from multiple research groups.
pulsars|Discovery of pulsars|1967|Astronomy|bell:Jocelyn Bell Burnell;hewish:Antony Hewish|pulsar|Britain||Bell identified unusual repeating radio signals during doctoral research. The discovery revealed pulsars, later understood as rotating neutron stars.
electroweak|Electromagnetism meets the weak force|1968|Atoms|salam:Abdus Salam;weinberg:Steven Weinberg;glashow:Sheldon Glashow|salam|Europe & United States||Glashow, Weinberg and Salam developed components of electroweak theory across the 1960s. It joined two interactions within a shared theoretical framework.
moon-landing|Human exploration of the Moon|1969|Engineering|johnson:Katherine Johnson|computing|United States||Apollo 11 placed humans on the Moon through the work of vast scientific and engineering teams. Johnson’s earlier trajectory work was one contribution within a much larger space programme.
restriction-enzymes|Molecular tools for cutting DNA|1970|Genetics|arber:Werner Arber;smith:Hamilton Smith;nathans:Daniel Nathans|restriction|United States & Switzerland||Research on restriction enzymes made it possible to cut DNA at particular sequences, supplying practical tools for gene analysis and engineering.
recombinant|Recombinant DNA methods|1973|Genetics|cohen:Stanley Cohen;boyer:Herbert Boyer;berg:Paul Berg|engineeringdna|United States||Researchers combined DNA from different sources and propagated it in cells. Related work by Berg, Cohen, Boyer and colleagues established genetic engineering methods.
dark-matter|Galaxy rotation & unseen mass|1970|Astronomy|rubin:Vera Rubin;ford:Kent Ford|cosmos|United States||Rubin and Ford’s galaxy rotation measurements joined other lines of evidence for unseen mass. The nature of dark matter remains unresolved.
ozone-cfc|CFCs threaten stratospheric ozone|1974|Climate|molina:Mario Molina;rowland:F. Sherwood Rowland;crutzen:Paul Crutzen|ozone|United States & Europe||Molina and Rowland linked chlorofluorocarbons to ozone destruction. Crutzen’s related atmospheric chemistry helped explain other catalytic ozone loss processes.
sanger-dna|Reading DNA sequences|1977|Genetics|sanger;gilbertseq:Walter Gilbert;maxam:Allan Maxam|sangerdna|Britain & United States||Sanger’s chain termination approach and a separate chemical method by Maxam and Gilbert enabled researchers to determine DNA base sequences.
ivf-birth|Birth after in vitro fertilization|1978|Medicine|edwards:Robert Edwards;steptoe:Patrick Steptoe;purdy:Jean Purdy|ivf|Britain||The birth of Louise Brown followed laboratory, clinical and embryology work by Edwards, Steptoe, Purdy and others, demonstrating successful IVF treatment.
hpylori|A bacterium linked to ulcers|1982|Medicine|marshall:Barry Marshall;warrenpath:Robin Warren|hpylori|Australia||Marshall and Warren connected Helicobacter pylori with gastritis and peptic ulcers, challenging prevailing explanations and changing treatment.
pcr|Amplifying DNA|1983|Genetics|mullis:Kary Mullis|pcr|United States||The polymerase chain reaction amplified selected DNA sequences. Practical development involved many colleagues and later use of heat stable polymerases.
hiv-discovery|Identifying HIV|1983|Medicine|barresinoussi:Françoise Barré Sinoussi;montagnier:Luc Montagnier|hiv|France||A research team isolated the virus later called HIV, providing an important step toward understanding AIDS and developing diagnostic tests.
backprop|Learning through backpropagation|1986|Computing|rumelhart:David Rumelhart;hinton:Geoffrey Hinton;williams:Ronald Williams|backprop|United States & Canada||An influential paper demonstrated learning internal representations using backpropagation. Related methods had earlier predecessors; this was a major development, not the sole invention.
web-invention|The World Wide Web proposed|1989|Computing|bernerslee:Tim Berners Lee;cailliau:Robert Cailliau|web|CERN||Berners Lee proposed a linked information system at CERN. Implementation and collaboration, including Cailliau’s contribution, brought the Web into operation.
human-genome|The Human Genome Project completed|2003|Genetics|collins:Francis Collins;sulston:John Sulston|hgp|International||An international public consortium completed its reference genome project. Many laboratories contributed; completion did not mean every human DNA region had been resolved.
blue-led|Efficient blue light emitting diodes|1993|Engineering|akasaki:Isamu Akasaki;amano:Hiroshi Amano;nakamura:Shuji Nakamura|led|Japan||Research breakthroughs by Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura enabled efficient blue LEDs. Blue light made practical white LED lighting possible with other components.
exoplanet-sun|A planet around a Sun like star|1995|Astronomy|mayor:Michel Mayor;queloz:Didier Queloz|exoplanet|Switzerland||Mayor and Queloz identified a planet around 51 Pegasi, a Sun like star. Earlier exoplanets had already been found around a pulsar.
quantum-teleport|Quantum teleportation demonstrated|1997|Quantum|zeilinger:Anton Zeilinger|entanglement|Austria||Experiments demonstrated transfer of a quantum state using entanglement and classical communication. This does not transport matter or permit faster than light messaging.
accelerating-universe|The universe’s expansion accelerates|1998|Astronomy|perlmutter:Saul Perlmutter;schmidt:Brian Schmidt;riess:Adam Riess|darkenergy|International||Independent supernova teams found evidence that cosmic expansion is accelerating. The result established a central observational basis for dark energy research.
rna-interference|RNA can silence genes|1998|Genetics|fire:Andrew Fire;mello:Craig Mello|rnai|United States||Experiments showed that double stranded RNA can cause sequence specific gene silencing, establishing RNA interference as a mechanism and research tool.
gps|Satellite navigation becomes operational|1995|Engineering|parkinson:Bradford Parkinson;gladys:Gladys West|relativity|United States||The GPS system reached full operational capability. Its accuracy depends on clocks, orbital models and relativistic corrections; West’s geodetic work was one of many enabling contributions.
protein-design|Designing a new protein|2003|Biology|baker:David Baker|protein|United States||Baker’s group demonstrated computational design of a novel protein, establishing a route toward engineering structures beyond those found in nature.
mrna-modified|Modified RNA reduces immune activation|2005|Medicine|kariko:Katalin Karikó;weissman:Drew Weissman|mrna|United States||Karikó and Weissman showed how nucleoside modifications can reduce unwanted immune responses to RNA, helping enable later mRNA medicines.
ips-cells|Reprogramming adult cells|2006|Biology|yamanaka:Shinya Yamanaka;takahashi:Kazutoshi Takahashi|stem|Japan||Defined factors converted mouse adult cells into induced pluripotent stem cells. Human cell demonstrations followed, changing possibilities for developmental research.
neanderthal-genome|A draft Neanderthal genome|2010|Genetics|paabo:Svante Pääbo|ancientdna|International||Ancient DNA research produced a draft Neanderthal genome and evidence of interbreeding, opening new ways to study human evolutionary history.
crispr-editing|Programmable CRISPR DNA cutting|2012|Genetics|doudna:Jennifer Doudna;charpentier:Emmanuelle Charpentier;jinek:Martin Jinek|crisprpaper|International||A team showed how CRISPR Cas9 could be programmed to cut DNA in vitro. This supplied a foundation for subsequent genome editing methods in cells.
alexnet|Deep learning changes image recognition|2012|Computing|krizhevsky:Alex Krizhevsky;sutskever:Ilya Sutskever;hinton|deeplearn|Canada||A neural network achieved a major improvement on an image recognition benchmark, combining training methods, large datasets and GPU computation.
higgs-detected|A Higgs boson discovered|2012|Atoms|higgs;englert|higgs|CERN||ATLAS and CMS reported a new boson consistent with the predicted Higgs particle. Thousands of researchers built and analysed the experiments; the theorists shown are not the experimental team.
gravity-waves|Gravitational waves observed|2015|Astronomy|weiss:Rainer Weiss;thorne:Kip Thorne;barish:Barry Barish|ligo|United States & International||LIGO detected spacetime waves from merging black holes in September 2015, announced in 2016. The result depended on a large international collaboration.
transformers|Attention based neural networks|2017|Computing|vaswani:Ashish Vaswani;shazeer:Noam Shazeer;parmar:Niki Parmar;uszkoreit:Jakob Uszkoreit;jones:Ll ion Jones;gomez:Aidan Gomez;kaiser:Łukasz Kaiser;polosukhin:Illia Polosukhin|transformer|International||The Transformer architecture used attention mechanisms without recurrent sequence processing, becoming an important foundation for later language and other machine learning models.
blackhole-image|Imaging a black hole’s shadow|2019|Astronomy||blackhole|International||The Event Horizon Telescope collaboration produced an image of the bright region around M87’s central black hole. This was a collective result spanning observatories worldwide.
protein-ai|AI predicts protein structures|2020|Computing|hassabis:Demis Hassabis;jumper:John Jumper|protein|Britain & International||AlphaFold2 made a major advance in predicting protein structures from sequences. Its predictions require scientific interpretation and do not resolve every biological question.
mrna-vaccine|mRNA vaccines enter widespread use|2020|Medicine|kariko;weissman|mrna|International||Vaccines based on mRNA were authorized for COVID 19. Earlier RNA science, delivery research and extensive development teams all contributed; the two researchers shown are foundational contributors.
complete-genome|A gapless human genome assembly|2022|Genetics|miga:Karen Miga;phillippy:Adam Phillippy|hgp|International||The Telomere to Telomere consortium published a complete assembly of a human genome, resolving many regions missing from earlier references. This is distinct from sequencing all human variation.
`;
const addedPeople=new Map();
function registerPerson(token,field,year,source,location){let [id,name]=token.split(':');if(!id)return null;if(!people.some(p=>p.id===id)&&!addedPeople.has(id)){if(!name)throw Error('Missing name for '+id);addedPeople.set(id,{id,title:name,subtitle:field+' researcher',field,year,source,location,text:'',contributions:[]})}return id;}
extraRows.trim().split('\n').forEach(row=>{const [id,title,year,field,names,source,location,approx,text]=row.split('|');if(!sources[source])throw Error('Missing source '+source);const scientistIds=names?names.split(';').map(t=>registerPerson(t,field,Number(year),source,location)):[];discoveries.push({id,title,year:Number(year),field,people:scientistIds,text,source,location,approx:!!approx});});
people.push(...addedPeople.values());
// Consistent subject naming and richer metadata on the original collection.
for(const d of discoveries){if(d.field==='DNA')d.field='Genetics';d.location??='Europe & North America';d.approx??=false;}
for(const p of people){if(p.field==='DNA')p.field='Genetics';p.contributions=discoveries.filter(d=>d.people.includes(p.id)).map(d=>d.id);p.year??=Math.min(...p.contributions.map(id=>discoveries.find(d=>d.id===id).year));if(!Number.isFinite(p.year))p.year=p.id==='davy'?1807:1900;p.location??=discoveries.find(d=>d.people.includes(p.id))?.location||'Europe';if(!p.text){const ds=p.contributions.map(id=>discoveries.find(d=>d.id===id));p.text='Contributed to '+ds.map(d=>d.title.toLowerCase()+' ('+(d.year<0?Math.abs(d.year)+' BCE':d.year)+')').join('; ')+'. Open each milestone for the contribution and its context.';}}
people.find(p=>p.id==='jones').title='Llion Jones';
